# Ford Consul Mk II four-door saloon, 1956-1962.
#
# The four-cylinder member of the 1956 'Three Graces' range, shorter in the
# bonnet than the Zephyr and Zodiac on the same 104 in wheelbase. 371,585
# fixed-roof cars and 9,398 convertibles built; renamed Consul 375 in 1961.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Consul
#
# Dimensions 4.369 x 1.753 x 1.524 m, wheelbase 2.642 m.
#
# Shape reference: no true level side elevation exists on Commons. Image 1 is
# a high rear three-quarter of a 1956 Highline car, about 25-30 degrees of
# square, and is NOT used as an elevation. The flank comes from that view and
# the front three-quarter, within the published dimensions; the limitation is
# recorded.
#
# Colours: grade (d) for a model-specific list. Grade (c) only for the later
# 1959-62 corporate palette. Savoy Black, Ermine White, Ambassador Blue and
# Goodwood Green are used, with the inference recorded. Weights 18, 16, 14,
# 12, total 60, provisional.
#
# Speed 135 km/h. Comfort 55/265, on the line 180 + 1.5 x 55: more room than
# the Mk I and a higher roofline. Classes 1,2.
# Dates are road presence: 1956 intro, retires staggered 1978-1981.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/ford-consul-mk2.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/ford-consul-mk2.dae. References:
# citycars/references/ford-consul-mk2/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: there is no true side elevation, so the flank is
# estimated from the high rear quarter. The rear window wrap and the lamp
# housings are simplified. At 128 px the high-roofed three-box body, broad grille
# and round lamps read as a Consul Mk II.


obj=citycar
